 "introductionText": "1. What FRUS answers directly. Across the whole printed series — all 553 volumes, of which about 6,700 documents fall under the editors' Cuba headings or name Cuba in their titles — the documents show the argument moving through four registers. In the 1870s Cuba mattered as a neighbour whose war, slavery and commerce spilled onto the United States: Secretary Fish wrote that the island was \"Commercially, as well as geographically\" closer to the United States than to Spain (Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1874, Document 552, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1874/d552). By 1897–98 the same disorder had become a threat to American property and to domestic peace, \"a constant menace to our peace\" in the President's 1898 message (Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1898, the President's message, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1898/ch83). From 1902 to the Second World War the security case was a protectorate's — intervention rights, Guantánamo, wartime air bases — and the prosperity case was sugar and tariffs, with Washington arguing in 1933 that better trade would calm Cuban politics (Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1933, The American Republics, Volume V, Document 241, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933v05/d241). After 1959 the two were fused and inverted: Cuba became a Soviet-aligned base for revolution and missiles, and the economy became a weapon to be turned against it (Foreign Relations of the United States, 1958–1960, Cuba, Volume VI, Document 517, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1958-60v06/d517; Foreign Relations of the United States, 1961–1963, Volume XI, Cuban Missile Crisis and Aftermath, Document 34, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1961-63v11/d34). By 1974 a State Department memorandum could say flatly that \"The United States has no vital interests in Cuba\" (Foreign Relations of the United States, 1969–1976, Volume E–11, Part 1, Document 278, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1969-76ve11p1/d278), and the Carter documents treat Cuba as a bargaining problem, Africa and refugees (Foreign Relations of the United States, 1977–1980, Volume XXIII, Mexico, Cuba, and the Caribbean, Documents 19 and 98, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1977-80v23/d19 and https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1977-80v23/d98).\n\n2. Where FRUS points for the rest. Series the printed documents came from (about 5,900 of the 6,700 carry a source note): the Department of State's Central Decimal File in Record Group 59, chiefly the classes for Cuba's internal political affairs, its finances, its agriculture (sugar) and U.S.–Cuban commerce, 1910–1963 (the bundled data name no identifier for the decimal file series itself); in RG 59 the lot files Records Relating to the Cuban Missile Crisis, 1962–1963 (entry A1 3053, NARA identifier 27022950, partly restricted), Records of Under Secretary George W. Ball, 1961–1966 (A1 5175, 614703, unrestricted), Files Pertaining to Operation Mongoose, 1963–1965 (UD-UP 293-B, 648748, unrestricted), the Assistant Secretary for Inter-American Affairs' Subject Files, 1959–1962 (A1 3147, 2068466, partly restricted), the Special Assistant on Communism's Subject Files, 1958–1961 (A1 3148, 2363836, partly restricted), and Records Relating to Activities with the National Security Council, 1948–1961 (A1 1583E/F, 2838992, partly restricted); and, outside RG 59, the Kennedy Library's National Security Files (collection 1164; Country Files 1961–1963, 193236), the Johnson Library's National Security File (567979; Latin America Country Files, 567985) and the Eisenhower Library's Whitman File (probably Papers as President, 1124), for which the bundled data give no access status; the Carter Library's National Security Affairs files did not resolve to an identifier. For the nineteenth century the shelves are RG 59's microfilmed Despatches from U.S. Consuls in Havana, 1783–1906 (file unit 196006797), Notes from the Cuban Legation, 1844–1906 (183303950), and Despatches from U.S. Ministers to Cuba, 1902–1906 (177380758), with no entry or access data bundled. Series the editors cited without printing (about 680 documents carry such citations, none before December 1910): RG 59's Records Relating to National Security Council Policy Papers, 1947–1979 (A1 1586E, 2839192, partly restricted), the most-cited lot; Presidential and Secretary of State Correspondence with Foreign Heads of State, 1953–1964 (A1 1590, 2538452); the Secretary's and Under Secretary's Memorandums of Conversation, 1953–1964 (A1 1566, 602231, unrestricted); Records Relating to Cuba, 1957–1975, of the Coordinator of Cuban Affairs (A1-5758, 1040618, partly restricted); and, again, the Missile Crisis and Ball lots and the Kennedy, Johnson and Carter national security files. Two lots that the documents cite did not resolve to any series.\n\n3. What this request cannot get from FRUS. \"National security\" as the policymakers' own concept: I searched the phrase in all 553 volumes. It appears in about 1,040 of the Cuba documents, but in only about 47 of them in the officials' own prose; in the rest it is the name of an archive or of the Council (the Kennedy Library's \"National Security Files\"). Its rate among the 1960s Cuba documents is no higher than in the whole 1960s corpus, so the phrase measures the series, not Cuba. The controls held: the phrase \"Department of State\" was found in all but one of the 553 volumes, and a nonsense term in none. \"Prosperity\": in no decade do more than 22 Cuba documents use the word, so it is not how anyone argued. The period's own words (commerce, property, sugar, tariffs, reciprocity) carry the economic case instead. The 1880s and the Ten Years' War seen from Havana: the editors filed this in the Spain chapters, and the Cuba headings hold almost nothing for 1870–1889. The shelf is the Havana consular despatches named above. Public, congressional and business opinion, and Cuban sources: FRUS prints government records. It cannot show what sugar interests, Congress or Cubans thought, except where a document quotes them.",
